WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.720
Welcome to Fascinating Frontiers, episode 108, coming to you on June 21, 2026.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:10.480
Here's your space and science briefing. The week's biggest developments pulled together,

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:16.860
what actually moved, why it matters, and what to watch next. Space X completed a funding round that

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:24.840
brought total capital raised to $85.7 billion. The round included a 20% increase in share value and

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:30.500
exercise of the green shoe option. Proceeds will support ongoing vehicle development and

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:37.200
constellation expansion. The valuation now places the company's market capitalization above $2.5

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.520
trillion. Investors are watching how the capital deploys across Starship and Starlink programs.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.980
Now we turn from private sector scale to the public telescopes revealing what that hardware will one

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:56.400
day study. The James Webb Space Telescope captured detailed atmospheric data showing silicate

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:03.180
clouds that condense into ruby and sapphire rain. Observations reveal temperature contrasts

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:09.280
and wind patterns driving the unusual precipitation cycle. The planet's atmosphere contains high

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:15.940
altitude clouds composed of minerals that would be gems on earth. With its unprecedented observational

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:21.680
quality, JWST gives us the most detailed glimpses into distant planets to date.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:26.380
Follow-up spectra are planned to track cloud evolution over multiple orbits.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.020
Shifting now to another James Webb discovery that rewrites the early universe picture.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.800
James Webb Space Telescope spectra show one of the mysterious early universe red dots,

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:44.860
is a compact object whose x-rays are absorbed by surrounding gas. The object's true nature only

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:50.820
appears once the dense cocoon is accounted for in the data reduction. Researchers had wondered whether

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:57.280
these abundant bright sources challenged standard galaxy formation models. The new observations instead

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:03.220
place them on the evolutionary track of growing supermassive black holes. Follow-up programs will

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:10.140
target additional red dots to test how common this disguise is at high redshift. From hidden black holes

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:16.220
we move to another hidden messenger. An ultra-high energy neutrino has been linked to a galaxy whose

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:22.500
jets point away from our line of sight. Researchers combined ice cube neutrino detections with radio

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:29.300
and gamma ray observations to identify the source. The galaxy's misaligned jets still produce neutrinos

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:34.940
through interactions and dense surrounding material. This detection adds to evidence that such

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:40.800
shadow blaster systems contribute to the cosmic neutrino flux. Follow-up monitoring will search

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.460
for additional neutrinos from the same direction. From cosmic messengers we turn to the steady cadence

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:53.440
of cargo flights keeping crews in orbit. The CRS-34 capsule splashed down after undocking from the

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:59.840
International Space Station. Four main parachutes deployed during reentry and the trunk was jettisoned

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:06.300
before landing. The mission delivered supplies and returned science samples. This flight continues the

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:12.120
steady cadence of commercial resupply flights. Next up is preparation for the following dragon mission

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:26.400
later this summer. The image shows CL0016 plus 1609 also known as maxj0018.125 plus 1626, one of the

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:33.340
latest X-ray clusters known. X-ray data revealed the system is two clusters aligned along our line of

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:40.300
sight and in the process of merging. The observations add to extensive radio and X-ray studies of the object.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.760
Hubble's visible light view highlights the distorted galaxies within the colliding system.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:52.640
Follow-up papers will examine how the merger affects galaxy evolution. While resupply runs keep

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:58.020
the station fed, NASA is preparing a new mission to study the space weather that can disrupt those

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:04.640
very satellites. NASA has selected the dynamic atmosphere ionosphere explorer mission concept to move

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:11.260
into phase B development. Daphne will measure how solar activity and atmospheric dynamics couple to

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:17.160
shape the near-earth space environment. Data from the mission are expected to improve forecasts that

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:23.840
protect GPS signals and satellites in low-earth orbit. Instruments will target energy transfer between

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.820
the lower atmosphere and the ionosphere. The selection follows a competitive review of proposals

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:35.780
addressing space weather impacts on technology. Researchers refined criteria for identifying

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:41.860
potentially habitable worlds around other stars. The updated framework incorporates atmospheric

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:48.400
retention and stellar activity levels. It reduces the list of high-priority targets for future telescopes.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:55.120
The work helps observers allocate limited observation time more effectively. Scientists will test

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:01.000
the model against new James Webb data releases. From near-earth weather, we move to the moon,

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:07.160
where long-term infrastructure rules are now being debated. NASA's plans for sustained lunar

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:12.280
presence require standardized construction rules to protect both hardware and future crews.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.720
A single expert has outlined how differing national standards could create incompatible

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:23.700
landing zones and habitats. The proposal draws on terrestrial building codes on accounts for lunar

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:30.220
dust, thermal extremes, and seismic activity from impacts. Without agreed rules, overlapping

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:35.960
infrastructure risks could delay Artemis follow-on missions. The discussion centers on technical

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:42.480
requirements rather than legal frameworks. From future lunar rules, we turn to an asteroid already

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:49.840
on its way for a close 2029 encounter. A spacecraft led by Tsinghua University will rendezvous with

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:56.860
asteroid Apophis during its 2029 Earth encounter. The mission joins planned efforts by other space

00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:03.920
agencies to study the 340 meter object at close range. Scientists aim to measure surface composition

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:10.540
and any structural changes induced by tidal forces. The university-developed probe will carry remote

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:16.480
sensing instruments tailored for the brief fly-by window. Launch timing remains under study to meet

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:23.700
the 2029 arrival. From one near-earth visitor, we turn to another. The Mars Express Orbiter captured

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:30.240
dozens of dust devils across the Mammars-Valez region. These whirlwinds appear as dark streaks or

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:36.580
bright plumes against the surface in high-resolution images. The observations help map local wind patterns

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:43.520
and dust-lifting processes in a mid-latitude valley system. Repeated imaging passes allow scientists

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:49.280
to track how frequently these vorces form. Future orbits will target seasonal changes in

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:56.500
devil activity. From Mars surface processes, we turn to a simple geometry trick on Venus. Venus rotates

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:04.760
once every 243 Earth days. A person walking westward at 6.5 kilometers per hour can match the planet's

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:11.400
spin. The steady pace holds the sun exactly on the horizon, turning sunset into a permanent view.

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:17.200
Planetary scientists note this works only because the atmosphere and surface move together

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:25.160
at that latitude. Observers on Earth can picture the geometry by comparing Venus' 243-day period

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:32.300
to our 24-hour cycle. From planetary oddities, we move to a faint cluster visible in Earth's own skies

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:40.960
tonight. The open cluster NGC-5897 sits nearly 12 degrees south of Beta Libre. At 10.30 pm,

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:46.100
local daylight time, the cluster lies highest in the southern sky for mid-northern observers.

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:51.080
Its loose structure and faint stars give it a ghostly appearance in small telescopes.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:58.200
No special equipment beyond binoculars is required under dark skies. The object remains observable

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:03.320
through the rest of June. From backyard viewing, we turn to the week's fresh launch activity.

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:09.800
Multiple Falcon 9 missions from Vandenberg confirmed 24 satellites starlink deployments.

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:17.240
The launches add to the ongoing constellation expansion cadence. A starlink group 17-28

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:25.520
launch occurred from Vandenberg at 1639 UTC. Deployment of 24 starlink satellites was confirmed across

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:32.240
the missions. Jackal satellite launch details formed part of the same activity. Keep an eye on the

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:38.300
next dragon mission scheduled for later this summer as preparation continues. That covers today's

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:43.180
space and science news. Share this with a fellow space enthusiast if you found it interesting.

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:47.980
I'm Patrick and Vancouver. See you tomorrow. And if you'd rather watch than listen,

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:53.260
find us on YouTube at Narra Network. Links in the show notes. And before you go,

